## 1. Introduction
Over the past several decades, the emission of carbon dioxide   
$(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ has raised continually, attributing to the growing   
dependence on the burning of fossil fuels (e.g. petroleum, coal,   
and natural gas).[1] According to the Intergovernmental Panel on   
Climate Change (IPCC), the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration in the atmosphere   
will reach approximately 570 ppm in 2100.[2] The high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ entration would not only increase the global average   
temperature, but also causes other serious problems such as   
ocean acidification, species extinction, desertification, etc.[2]   
Hence, it is urgent to pursuit new technologies and methods to   
reduce the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission, thereby decreasing these irreversible   
environmental deteriorations.[3-5] Recently, researchers have put   
forward some practical strategies to address this issue. The   
strategies mainly include carbon capture and storage (CCS)   
technologies and conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into high value-added   
chemicals and fuels. Relative to CCS technologies, the strategy of   
converting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into high value-added chemicals is a more conomical method to reduce the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ emission, which has been   
considered as a more sustainable way and conforms to the   
concept of green development.[6] Up to now, sorts of   
heterogeneous catalysts were employed to convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into high   
value chemicals including metal oxides,[7] molecular sieve,[8]   
silica-supported metal salt,[9] and porous polymer,[10] etc. Among
these catalysts, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with potential applications in the areas of drug delivery,[11-12] proton conductivity,[13-14] catalysis,[15-19] adsorption and separation,[20-25] have been considered as one of the most promising materials for the application of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion. On the one hand, MOFs possess some advantageous features including intrinsic crystallinity, high porosity, and controllable structure, abundant active sites, etc., which would benefit for structural design and functional assembly of catalysts. On the other hand, the outstanding ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability of MOFsto improve the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentration around the catalytic active center, thereby promoting the conversion efficiency.[26] In this point, our group and other researchers have developed sorts of MOFs for capturing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2},$ which shows remarkable ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity and selectivity.[27-36] The high capacity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ makes these MOFs to be potential candidates for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion.
To date, several reviews have systematically summarized the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion applications of MOFs. For example, Liang et $a I$ [37] reviewed the fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into cyclic carbonates by MOFs. Ding et al.[38] reported the carbon conversion by MOFs from the view of the design and synthesis of MOF-based materials and their catalytic performances for the fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Distinct from previous reviews, this review focuses on the investigation of MOF catalysts in preparing different chemical products (Scheme 1) including fine chemicals (cyclic carbonate, alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters, carboxylic propargyl amine, etc.) and some small molecule compounds (carbon monoxide, methane, formate, acetate, etc.) by using ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ as C1 source. We hope this review will provide a direct guide in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion from the view of target products and the corresponding design of MOF catalysts.
## 2. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ to fine chemicals
## 2.1. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ to cyclic carbonate by MOFs
Cyclic carbonate is an important industrial raw material, which can be used in high energy battery electrolyte,[39-42] fine chemical intermediate,[43,44] and a polar solvent.[45,46] One of the most important ways to synthesize cyclic carbonate is
