cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and epoxide, in which these raw materials are low in price and high in atomic utilization, in accordance with the concept of green chemistry.[47-50] The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction is generally considered to be activated by acid sites or acid-base synergistic sites.[51,52] Here we classify these catalytic reactions according to the types of MOF acidic sites.
## 2.1.1. Lewis acid sites based on monometallic nodes
Zeolite imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are one of the most representative MOFs with monometallic node. Their excellent thermal and chemical stability make them be an excellent choice as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition catalysts.[53-57] As early as 2010, Chizallet et $a^{\prime}.$ [57] proved that $Z n^{2+}$ ions serve as strong Lewis acid sites and oexist on the surface of ZIF-8 crystals. Moreover, the presence of basic nitrogen atoms in imidazole ligand favors the binding and ctivation of the polar carbon−oxygen bonds of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Therefore, the synergistic effect is observed among them and the material hows great potential in catalyzing the cycloaddition $\mathsf{o f C O}_{2}$ to epichlorohydrin. In 2012,[58] Carreon et al. reported the conversion of chloroethylene oxide to cyclic carbonate by ZIF-8 Scheme 2). $Z n^{2+}$ ions in ZIF-8 serve as Lewis acid sites. Moreover, ethylenediamine (ED) was introduced into the framework to act Lewis base sites. Control experiments showed that the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of amine-functionalized ZIF-8 was higher than that of parent ZIF-8. They claimed that the introduction of amines in ZIF-8 could promote the adsorption and activation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and make the reaction more efficient. In the reaction system, the synergistic effects of both Lewis acid and Lewis base sites romote the catalytic reaction. However, the ED modified ZIF-8 exhibited poor cycling performance. The reason can be attributed o the fact that the produced carbonaceous during the reaction blocked the pores in the ZIF-8. In 2013, Carreon’s group[59] further investigated the catalytic activity of ZIF-8 for the conversion of C $\O_{2}$ . Even if the low styrene carbonate yields of $\sim54\%$ , ZIF-8 could be reused for three cycles. After that, a higher yield $(93.3\%)$ of yrene carbonate was achieved in Yu’s group[60] by using ZIF-68 as a heterogeneous catalyst. They claimed that the high yields of yrene carbonate may due to the synergistic effect between Lewis acid sites of $Z n^{2+}$ and Lewis basic sites in imidazolate ligand, ell as the contribution of the nitro group to increase the interactions between ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and MOF framework. Although ZIF-68 exhibits higher catalytic activity in producing styrene carbonate, the poor recyclability is still a tough problem. The reasons could also be ascribed to the produced product matter that blocked the pores of ZIF-68.
As we all know, recyclability is a very important parameter of any catalyst. Therefore, it is very important to design suitable MOF catalysts with excellent catalytic activity as well as recyclability. From the point of structure view, due to the catalytic tes are spread inside and outside the surface of crystals, the pores of MOFs could be blocked by products. Hence, it is necessary to design MOF materials with large pore size, that would make the reactants and products pass through the pore freely.
Scheme 2  The conversion of epichlorohydrin to chloropropene carbonate by ZIF-8. Reprinted with permission from ref. 58, Copyright 2011, American Chemical Society.
To solve the problem of poor cycling performance, researchers have made massive efforts. In 2015,[61] Park et al. used zinc (II) nitrate hexahydrate and imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde as raw materials to synthesize ZIF-90, which was used as a catalyst for the cycloaddition of propylene oxide (PO) with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The result showed that ZIF-90 can be reused for 5 times. In addition, the reaction can be conducted without using cocatalysts and organic solvents. Moreover, the catalytic mechanism was investigated by Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) calculations (Scheme 3), which indicated the strong interactions between $Z n^{2+}$ (Lewis acid center) and oxygen atoms of propylene oxide, suggesting $Z n^{2+}$ sites to be one of the important catalytic sites. Meanwhile, the aldehyde group of ZIF-90 can activate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to form a ring-opening intermediate and then turn off the loop to form a cyclic carbonate. A similar reaction system was reported by the Verpoort’s group in 2016, [62] they employed ZIF-67 to efficiently convert epoxide to cyclic carbonate. They concluded that ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ in ZIF-67 acts as a Lewis acid to activate epoxides, while imidazole nitrogen atoms as a Lewis base to activate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The synergistic effect between Lewis acid and Lewis base in ZIF-67 can efficiently induce the reaction to proceed.
