Even if sole ZIF materials can catalyze the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction, but the problems of moderate yield still need to be solved. Therefore, cocatalysts were used in the monometallic MOF catalytic system for improving the catalytic performance on converting epoxide to cyclic carbonate.[63,64] For example, Park’s group[5] used 4-azabenzimidazole and zinc nitrate to synthesize ZIF-23. The tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBAB) was used as a cocatalyst combined with ZIF-23 to catalyze the reaction of propylene epoxide with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . A higher yield of $90\%$ was obtained in ZIF-23/TBAB reaction system. And such a catalytic system can onduct four cycles. They concluded that the synergistic effects of the catalysis system mainly result from the Lewis acid from $Z n^{2+}$ , polarization ability of pyridine N atoms, and nucleophilicity of TBAB (Scheme 4).
The similar synergistic effect of cocatalyst can also be observed in the ZIF-95 system, in which the addition of TBAB cocatalyst can reduce the reaction temperature.[65] Indeed, the introduction of nucleophilic sites in the reaction system can fficiently promote the catalytic performance of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition. However, the addition of cocatalysts in the system will increase the operating cost. Besides, it needs to separate the product and cocatalyst after the reaction, making the synthesis ess more complicated. In this point, Park group’s [66,67] developed ionic liquid and quaternary ammonium functionalized ZIF-90 (IL-ZIF-90 and F-ZIF-90) as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition catalysts. The introduction of ionic liquid and quaternary ammonium onto ZIF-90 framework will combine the function of metal Lewis acid ites $(Z\mathsf{n}^{2+})$ and nucleophilic I- sites, thus increasing the activity of catalysts. The obtained catalysts show an excellent yield of $95.0\%$ and $96.6\%$ for producing cyclic carbonate by IL-ZIF-90 and -ZIF-90, respectively. Due to the linkage of nucleophilic sites onto the MOF, the catalyst can be easily recycled compared with cocatalyst systems.
Besides the effects of nucleophilic group, the morphology of catalysts is verified to be another important factor to affect catalytic performance. In 2019, Hu and coworkers[68] synthesized ZIF-8 with different morphologies including solid spheres, hollow spheres, hierarchical particles, and stackable particles (Figure 1).
The results showed that ZIF-8 solid spheres possess the highest specific surface areas and most abundant acid-base sites, accounting for its superior catalytic activity. Furthermore, ZIF-8 solid spheres exhibit excellent recyclability, which can keep its activity after five cycles.
In a word, MOFs with monometallic node have been one kind of effective catalysts in converting epoxide and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to cyclic carbonate. Their performance can be adjusted by metallic Lewis acid sites, Lewis base sites of imidazolate ligands, nucleophilic sites of cocatalysts/ionic liquid/quaternary ammonium halogen anion, and as well as the morphology of MOFs. However, these catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion still face difficulties of poor recyclability, harsh reaction conditions, and moderate yield. It will thus affect the efficiency and recyclability of catalysts.
## 2.1.2. Lewis acid sites based on metal cluster nodes
Metal cluster is another kind of MOF nodes with Lewis acidity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition. To data, several kinds of metal clusters embedded in MOFs have been extensively studied as a promising catalyst for the conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ such as $Z n_{4}0_{\cdot}$ ${\mathsf{C u}}_{2}({\mathsf{C O O}})_{4},$ $\mathsf{M}_{3}\mathsf{F O}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6},\mathsf{Z r}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{8},\mathsf{M}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{3}(\mathsf{C O O})_{3},\mathsf{M O}_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{2},$ etc. An important advantage of metal cluster-based MOFs compared with ZIF materials is the wider window sizes and more Lewis acid sites, which would improve the catalytic efficiency of MOFs in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition. Thus, we will make a brief introduction from the perspective of distinct metal clusters as catalytic active centers in the following section.
## 2.1.2.1. $z_{n_{4}0}$ cluster
The $Z n_{4}O$ cluster is a representative metal node of the isoreticular metal-organic frameworks (IRMOF) series, usually composed of four $Z n^{2+}$ and one $\mu_{4}–0^{2-}$ . Each $Z n^{2+}$ coordinated with one $\mu_{4}–0^{2-}$ and three carboxylic acid ions, respectively, presenting four coordination configurations with potential Lewis acid catalytic activity.[69,70] In 2009, Han and researchers[71] firstly used MOF-5/TBAB catalytic system to catalyze ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition with
