propylene oxide. The structure of the MOF-5 framework is shown in Figure 2. In the reaction system, the $Z n_{4}O$ clusters serve as Lewis acidic sites strongly interact with the oxygen atom of the epoxide and activate the epoxy ring (Scheme 5). Then the Br(from $\scriptstyle n-B\cup\mathsf{N}_{4}\mathsf{B r})$ attacks the carbon atom of the coordinated epoxides, followed by the ring-opening step. Finally, the oxygen anion interacts with the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to lead to the form of alkylcarbonate anion, which is converted into the corresponding cyclic carbonate through the ring-closing step. The results showed that the wo-component catalyst exhibits high catalytic activity with $97.6\%$ yield under mild conditions. The reason mainly results from the ynergistic effect between MOF-5 and $\scriptstyle n-B u N_{4}\mathsf{B r}$ . More importantly, the results showed that there is no significant decrease in activity after cycling 3 times. Meantime, Baiker and coworkers[72] reported a mixed-linker MOF with the general formula of $Z n_{4}O(B D C)_{\times}(A B D C)_{3-x},$ which was used to catalyze $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition with propylene oxide. Under the same conditions, the introduction of the amino group on the MOF framework exhibited higher catalytic activity compared with MOF-5. They claimed that the amino side group and Zn sites at the surface will play the role of activating ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and propylene oxide, respectively.
Scheme 5  The plausible reaction mechanism for the cycloaddition of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with epoxides catalyzed by MOF-5 and $\scriptstyle n-\mathsf{B u}_{4}\mathsf{N B r}$ . Reprinted with permission from ref. 71, Copyright 2009, Royal Society of Chemistry.
A similar example was reported by Park’s group[73] in 2016. They employed 2-aminoterephthalic acid and 1,3,5-Tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene to react with zinc nitrate to achieve a micro-mesoporous MOFs, UMCM- $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ . The preparation process of UMCM-1- $\cdot N H_{2}$ is shown in Scheme 6. In the catalyst, micropores are predominantly region of catalytic sites, while mesopores allow easy guest diffusion and molecular accessibility. The results exhibited that the catalytic system can efficiently catalyze the synthesis of cyclic carbonate esters under mild conditions ( $25^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $1.2\mathsf{M p a}\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ . They concluded that the multi-stage pore structure in UMCM-1 $-N H_{2}$ plays a very important role in the reaction activity.[73]
The above reports provide direct evidence that $Z n_{4}O$ clusters in MOFs can play the role of Lewis acid to catalyze ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction. However, the cocatalyst must be used in the reaction system, which is a serious drawback for their applications in the synthesis of cyclic carbonate.
## 2.1.2.2. $M_{2}(C O O)_{4}$ and $N_{2}O(C O O)_{2}$ cluster
