## 2.1.2.4. $M_{3}F O(C O O)_{6}$ cluster
Trivalent metals cluster $(\mathsf{M}_{3}\mathsf{F O}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6},$ where $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C}\mathsf{r}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , etc.) with strong Lewis acid activity can also be used to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition. A typical MOF based on trivalent metals cluster is MIL-101-Cr,[82] in which consists of three $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ , six COO-, one $\mu_{3}–0^{2\cdot}$ , one F-, and two ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ molecules. Where each ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{r}}^{3+}$ is coordinated with four carboxylate oxygen, one $\mu_{3}–0^{2\cdot}$ and $H_{2}O/F\cdot$ , respectively, presenting the octahedral coordination configuration. Water molecules linked to $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ can be removed by activation to expose unsaturated metal sites, thus giving rise to a Lewis acid activity. In 2013, Zalmaeva et al.[83] reported the application of MIL-101-Cr in converting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and epoxides to cyclic carbonate. The results exhibited that propylene oxide and styrene oxide can be efficiently converted into the corresponding carbonate with high yields under high pressure and cocatalyst. To obtain bifunctional catalysts with the excellent catalytic performance for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition, in 2015, Shi’s group[84] developed a strategy for combining coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (CUS) with ionic liquid functional site (Figure 7). The as-constructed MIL-101-N(n-Bu)3Br and MIL-101- $P(n-B u)_{3}B r$ showed outstanding catalytic activity for the cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and epoxide. Especially, the obtained MIL-101- $N(n-B u)_{3}B r$ exhibits significantly high activity for coupling of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and propylene oxide to form propylene carbonate with a yield of $99.1\%$ . Importantly, the catalyst can be conducted without the requirement of cocatalyst and it can be recycled for three times without loss in activity. The reason for this is that there is a synergistic effect of ${C r^{3+}}$ and $B r^{-}$ , result in the bifunctional catalysts exhibit excellent catalytic performance. Such a strategy can be expanded to introduce other types of ion liquids into MOFs and to construct catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation.
