After that, a series of metalloporphyrin based MOFs were employed to catalyze the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction such as OF-525, PCN-222, PCN-224, [91] $\mathrm{[\{Mn(TCPP)0.5(H_{2}O)_{2}\}{\cdot}2H_{2}O]_{n},^{\ell}}$ 2] CPM-13,[93] etc. These catalysts exhibit superior catalytic activity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation.
Macrocycle ligands with extra metal-chelating sites were also used to construct high active ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition catalysts. In 2014, Ma’s group[94] prepared a MOF material (MMCF-2) with the same nbo topology as MOF-505 (Figure 9). The structural analysis showed that the framework of MMCF-2 was composed of twelve $\angle121(\mathsf{C O O})_{4}$ clusters with vertices fourteen-sided cage structure and nitrogen heterocyclic tetracarboxylic acid tactmb as bridging ligands (tactmb $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 1,4,7,10-tetrazazcyclododecane-N,N',N'',N'''-tetra- $p$ -methylbenzoi c acid). The $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ions coordinated with tactmb ligands can act as additional catalytic active sites. Therefore, MMCF-2 with bimetallic catalytic sites can catalyze various epoxides into cyclic rbonate with a yield of $37.6{\sim}95.4\%$ under mild condition (ambient temperature and pressure). The catalytic activity of MMCF-2 is significantly higher than that of MOF-505, HKUST, and u-tactmb. However, the requirement of cocatalyst in the reaction system is a small drawback and thus limits its practice cation.
Another way to introduce dual metal sites in MOF is to construct heterometallic MOF. In 2020, Wang’s group[95] prepared a new heterometallic 3D MOF via the $H_{2}L$ (N,O-mixed 2-(imidazol-1-yl)terephthalic acid) ligand and ${\mathsf{P b}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ as well as $Z n(11)$ , namely $\{[\mathsf{P b}2\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{L})_{2}]\cdot\mathsf{D M A}\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{\mathsf{n}}$ (HMOF 2) with unsaturated Pb(II) and $Z n(11)$ active sites (Figure 10a). Due to the existence of the abundant unsaturated metal sites in HMOF 2, it could be employed as a catalyst together with TBAB for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition with various epoxides (Figure 10b). Notably, the reaction yield can reach up to $97\%$ under moderate conditions. This work provides an effective strategy for the design of effective d-p HMOF catalysts. In the HMOF 2/TBAB system, the epoxide was activated by the unsaturated bimetal nodes of HMOF 2 via the interactions of the coordination bonds. Furthermore, the Br- of TBAB attacked carbon atoms to activate the substrates, therefore resulting in the ring-opening of the substrates to form the active oxygen anions. Then the active oxygen anion reacted with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to engender alkylcarbonate anions. Finally, the corresponding cyclic carbonate and the catalyst were generated by a ring-closing step (Scheme 10).[96,97]
