In addition, Jiang et al. prepared hollow porous carbons (HPC) with well dispersed dopants of nitrogen and single Zn atoms through the template growth and pyrolysis method. The results showed that the optimized HPC-800 exhibited excellent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition with epoxides under light irradiation. They ascribed he reasons to the ultrahigh loading of single-atom Zn and uniform N active sites. Moreover, the hierarchical pores in the carbon shell are also beneficial for the high efficiency in catalyzing $C O_{2}$ cycloaddition. This work provides the first example on the ntegration of the photothermal effect of HPC together with single metal atoms for catalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation. [98] The cooperation of multiple sites in MOFs also benefits the catalytic performance. Furthermore, Jiang’s group also developed multiple sites MOF for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition. They prepared ZnO nanoparticles encapsulated N-doped porous carbon through the combination treatment of pyrolysis and moderate oxidation. The results showed that the as-prepared MOF exhibits outstanding catalytic performance, selectivity, and recyclability in the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions ith epoxides.[99] Theoretically, any MOFs with unsaturated coordination points (monometallic ions, metal clusters, or multi-metal sites) and potential Lewis acid catalytic activity can be mployed to catalyze ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction. However, it should be noted that the performance of MOF catalysts will be affected by sorts of factors such as window size, the density of ewis acid, the synergistic effect of functional sites such as Lewis base sites and nucleophilic sites, framework stability, and reaction conditions, etc. Therefore, some optimized catalysts still need to be designed and prepared to meet the industrial requirement for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ cycloaddition.
2.2. MOFs Conversion of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ to alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters by MOFs
Carboxylic acids are important starting materials/intermediates in organic synthesis.[100] Silver ions (or silver nanoparticles) are commonly used to promote the carboxylation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with the terminal alkyne and finally obtain the alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters.[ 101] Take the full advantage of MOFs, the preparation of the hybrid $\mathsf{A g@M O F}$ composites could be an excellent strategy to converse ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters.
In 2015, Cheng’s group[102] prepared Ag@MIL-101 composite by loading silver nanoparticles on MIL-101-Cr using a simple liquid impregnation method (Figure 11). Ag@MIL-101 can catalyze 1-ethynylbenzene and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to afford 3-phenylpropiolic with a yield of $96.5\%$ under mild conditions. And it can be regenerated and reused at least five times. They believed that the high activity of Ag@MIL-101 is mainly based on the following: (1) Ag nanoparticle with small size can well be dispersed in mesoporous cavities in MIL-101; (2) Ag@MIL-101 can effectively capture ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and improve ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration nearby the catalytic center to facilitate the reaction; (3) Lewis acid sites of coordination unsaturated ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ can preferentially adsorb aromatic substrates, thus enhancing the activity of the catalysts.
In 2019, Wei et al.[103] prepared $A g@Z|F-8$ materials by contacting ZIF-8 with ${\sf A g N O}_{3}$ in methanol for $30\mathrm{min}$ (Scheme 11). The materials were employed as catalysts for carboxylation of terminal alkynes via direct insertion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to the C(sp)-H bond. An optimized yield of $97\%$ was achieved in the synthesis of 3-phenylpropiolic acid by $0.5\mathsf{A g}@\mathsf{Z l F}8$ under $40^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and 1 atm. In the reaction system, $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ nanoparticles were responsible for alkynes activation and the highly porous framework provided a strong affinity toward ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Such catalysts can be reused for four runs. Moreover, a series of MOFs have been also reported as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion catalysts, such as Ag/Co-MOF,[104] Pd-Cu/MIL-101,[105] Ag@MIL-100(Fe),[106] and $\mathsf{A g@U l O-66(Z r),}_{}^{[106]}$ etc. These materials show the synergistic effect of MOF frameworks with metal nanoparticles (NPs).
