## 3. Conversion of $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ to carboxylic propargyl amine by MOFs
In 2018, Duan et al.[107] prepared two new silver cluster-based MOFs by utilizing the thiourea functionalized tripodal ligands. The multiple H-bonding groups on the ligands accelerated the capture and activation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and propargyl-amines. The substrate is limited to terminal alkynes, and phenyl substituted alkynes do not react due to their large steric resistance. The same year, Fei et al.[108] used ${\mathsf{C u}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2},$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (bpy), 1,2,4,5-benzenetetramethanesulfonate (1,2,4,5-BTMS) as raw reagents, synthesized a non-interpenetrated organosulfonate-based MOF $\mathsf{\{C u(b p y)_{2}(1,2,4,5-B T M S)_{0.5}(H_{2}O)_{0.5}]_{n}}}$ (defined as TMOF-3) with a defective pcu topology through solvothermal method (Scheme 12). The results showed that the cyclic carboxylation of alkyne molecules into $\alpha$ -alkylidene cyclic arbonates and oxazolidinones are successfully catalyzed by using $\mathsf{A g}(|)$ -embedded sulfonate-MOF under atmospheric pressure of $C\mathsf{2}_{2}$ . However, the internal propiolic alcohol can only get the orresponding carbonate with a yield of $25\%$ , because the steric resistance from the internal propiolic alcohol makes it difficult for he dispersion of substrates into MOF channels, and the $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ site is insufficient for the internal alkyne $\pi$ activation.
In 2018, Hou et al.[109] synthesized a noble-metal-free MOF with large 1D channels of $1.66\mathsf{n m}$ in diameter under environmentally friendly conditions, $\{(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6})_{0.75}[\mathsf{C u}_{4}|_{4}(\mathsf{L})_{3}(\mathsf{I n})_{0.75}]\cdot\mathsf{D M F}\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}$ ( $L=5$ -aminonicotinic acid, the as-synthesized compound defined as compound 1), as catalysts for the cyclization of propargylic alcohols with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ (Figure 12). The obtained compound 1 was stable in the corrosive triethylamine over one month and showed the promising acid/base stability. It is worth to mention that the catalytic reaction can be carried out without any solvents. Note, this MOF catalyst also has rarely catalytic activity toward ethisterone. The authors concluded that the synergistic effect between Cu(I) and In(III) plays a key role in the reaction.
In 2019, Zhao et al.[110] designed the metal-free MOF to catalyze the cycloaddition of alkynylpropanol with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . They used CuI, $\mathsf{I n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3},$ and 5-aminopyridine-3-carboxylic acid as raw materials synthesized a case of heterometallic MOFs with open nanoporous channels under solvothermal conditions. The results showed that the MOFs can effectively catalyze the carboxylation cyclization of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with various propargylic alcohols. They proved that the $[\mathsf{C u}_{4}|_{4}]$ cluster in the MOFs nanochannel is the key catalytic site, and $\mathsf{I n}^{3+}$ is the co-catalyst. Both $[\mathsf{C u}_{4}|_{4}]$ cluster and $\ln^{3+}$ can efficiently promote the reaction.
In general, the reaction mechanisms of MOF in the conversion of carbon dioxide could be summarized from the material structures. The metal ions in MOFs can serve as an unsaturated Lewis acid site. Some basic groups could be introduced into the MOFs to act as Lewis base sites. The synthetic effect of the Lewis acid and base site could efficiently enhance the activities of MOFs in the conversion of carbon dioxide. Moreover, MOFs with unsaturated Lewis acid sites can also be used together with some co-catalyst such as tetrabutyl ammonium bromide (TBAB).
