Generally, the introduction of TBAB in the Lewis acid MOF systems will play a more important role compared with the strategy of introducing basic group solely into MOFs. However, the TBAB is a homogeneous catalyst exhibiting an environmentally unfriendly feature. Therefore, the design of MOF catalysts with high activity and environmentally friendly features is still a tough problem.
## 3. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ to small-molecule compounds
As we mentioned above that the MOF can serve as the catalyst to converse ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to some fine chemicals. Besides fine chemicals, MOFs can be utilized to produce some useful small molecule compounds by the photocatalytic or electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction.[111-116] In the next part, we will make a brief introduction.
## 3.1. MOFs for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ reduction
Recently, the utilization of solar energy for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation becomes a hot topic. Sunlight-driven photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reactions can not only reduce the green-house effect but also relieve the energy shortage.[38,117-119] MOFs have recently emerged as photo-catalytic materials for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction because of their fascinating features, such as abundant ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption sites and tunable pore sizes.[120-122] MOFs could either serve as the photoactive materials or co-catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Due to the presence of organic ligands, MOFs could also be employed as semiconductors. Therefore, to realize the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, the conduction band (CB) of the MOFs must be more negative than the reduction semireaction potential. Moreover, the valence band (VB) must be more positive than oxidation semireaxtion potential.[122] To date, the main photochemical products catalyzed by MOFs can mainly be divided into gaseous molecules (e.g. CO, $\therefore H_{4},$ , etc.) as well as liquid molecules (e.g. HCOOH, ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{O H}}$ , etc.).[123-126]
## 3.1.1. Conversion of $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ to gaseous small-molecules
## 3.1.1.1. Conversion of $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ to CO
Up to now, some MOFs have been studied for the conversion $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{U}1}\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{\hookrightarrow}}\cup_{2}$ to CO by photochemical reduction. For example, Zhang’s group[127] used a cobalt MOF MAF-X27-OH as photocatalyst for he ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction under visible light in pure ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ of 1.0 atm by using $\mathsf{\Lambda}[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ as the photosensitizer (Figure 13). The results showed that catalytic active metal center can cooperate ith neighboring ${\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ to promote the photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. The as-prepared catalyst showed CO selectivity and turnover frequency (TOF) up to $98.2\%$ and $28\times10^{-3}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ , espectively. The author proved that the $\mu{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}^{\mathrm{-}}$ ligands serve as strong hydrogen-bonding donors to stabilize the initial $C_{0^{-}}\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adduct and local proton sources and thus facilitate the C-O bond breaking. And this will result in a weak $H_{2}O$ binding affinity toward ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , thus decreasing the production of ${\sf H}_{2}$ byproduct. Furthermore, MAF-X27-OH possessed high stability and can reuse three runs.
Another cobalt MOF (namely ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}–{\mathsf{M O F}}$ ) reported by Wang and researchers[128] was also used in photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. The $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}(\mathsf{N T B})(4,4^{\prime}\mathsf{-b p y})_{1.5}]$ ·DEF $\vert N T B=$ $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -nitrilotribenzoic acid ligands ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6^{-}}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ ) was constructed by linking $\mathsf{C o}_{6}(\mu_{3}{-}0\mathsf{H})_{6}$ clusters with $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -nitrilotribenzoic acid and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine to form a tfz- $\pmb{d}$ topology (Figure 14). The ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6^{-}}{\mathsf{M O F}}$ was used to catalyze ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction under visible light by using $\mathsf{\Lambda}[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ as the photosensitizer. The catalytic results showed that $39.36\mu\mathrm{mol}$ CO and $28.13\mu\mathrm{mol}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ could be obtained under visible-light irradiation for 3 hours. And they proposed possible mechanism as follows: (1) after irradiation, the photosensitizer $\mathsf{\Lambda}[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ is excited to the excited state, and then the electrons in the reduced photosensitizer are transferred to $\mathsf{C o}_{6}$ -MOF; (2) the adsorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule on $\mathsf{C o}_{6}$ -MOF is activated and further reduced to CO.
