Conductive MOF materials were also employed for the photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . In 2018, Zhu et al.[129] used the exfoliated nanosheets of conductive 2D-MOF $N i_{3}(H\mathsf{I T P})_{2}$ ( ${\mathsf{H I T P}}=$ 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene) as an efficient catalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, in which the $\mathsf{\Lambda}[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ acts as photosensitizer (Figure 15). The catalytic results showed that the hybrid hotocatalytic system exhibits an excellent selectivity of $97\%$ toward CO and a high CO yield rate of $3.45\times10^{4}\mu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}.$ . The ason for the high activity could be attributed to the synergistic effects of high conductivity, the nanosheet morphology, and the high active Ni catalytic center coexisted in $N i_{3}(H\mathsf{I T P})_{2}$ .
Another effective strategy for boosting the photocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction was to construct a hydrophobic nvironment in the MOF framework. For instance, in 2019, Chen’s group[130] introduced the methylene groups around the metal enter in MCF-55-Ni, which is synthesized by reacting 1,4,7,10-tetrazazcyclododecane-N,N',N'',N'''-tetra- $\cdot\mathsf{p}$ -methylbenzoi c acid with $N i C l_{2}\cdot6H_{2}O$ . The results showed that MCF-55-Ni exhibited high catalytic activity $(\mathsf{T O F}=73.8\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , selectivity $=96\%$ ) otocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into CO due to the methylene groups surrounding the metal center of MOF provide hydrophobic repulsive forces to ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ .
Furthermore, introducing semiconductors into MOFs systems is also an effective way to enhance the catalytic activity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hotoreduction. For example, Wang’s group[131] employed CdS semiconductor and Co-ZIF-9 to construct a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction system. This hybrid system exhibited high catalytic activity in deoxygenative conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. During three hours, the accumulated yield of CO and ${\sf H}_{2}$ can reach to $85.6\mu\mathrm{mol}$ and 38.8 mol, respectively. They proposed a possible reaction mechanism, as illustrated in Scheme 13. Firstly, the electron-hole pairs were generated on the CdS semiconductor under visible light irradiation; secondly, the visible light-induced electrons (e-) migrated to the conduction band of CdS and then transferred to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule adsorbed by Co-ZIF-9. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ intermediates were formed and stabilized by the benzimidazole linkers and then reduced to CO; meantime, the protons could also be directly reduced by the excited electrons and thus formed ${\sf H}_{2}$ byproduct.
Carbon nitride $(\mathsf{g}{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ presenting high photocatalytic activities is another kind of semiconductor under visible-light, but the low capacity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ limited its further application.[132-134] While MOFs with abundant adsorption sites are expected to be the promising materials for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion. Note, the use of MOFs can make up for the deficiency of $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheet (CNNS). In 2015, Shi et al.[135] prepared a hybrid photocatalyst UiO-66/CNNS through an electrostatic self-assembly method (Scheme 14). The results showed that UiO-66/CNNS not only exhibits higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption than the sole CNNS but also shows excellent catalytic activity for photocatalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO, which can achieve a CO yield of $59.4\mu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}_{\mathsf{c N}}{}^{-1}$ under light irradiation for 6 hours. The high catalytic activity can attribute to the fact that the composite of ${\tt g}{-}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ with UiO-66 suppresses the electron-hole pair recombination in $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-C}}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheet.
