## 3.1.1.2. Conversion of $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$
Methane can be used as fuel and raw materials for producing carbon black, acetylene, hydrocyanic acid, and so on.[143-146] MOFs with rich porosity and great structural diversity have attracted ${\mathfrak{m}}\ u{\mathfrak{c h}}$ attention and have emerged as photocatalysts for the photochemical reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ under visible light. For example, in 2014, Xiong and coworkers[147] integrated the inorganic semiconductor with MOFs to obtain a hybrid composite. The composite materials provided an excellent platform for gaseous photocatalytic reactions (Figure 18). The obtained $J_{2}(B\mathsf{T C})_{2}\textcircled{\varpi}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ shows high activity for converting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ under UV illumination and the yield is five times higher than bare $\phantom{}_{11}0_{2}$ . Because the production of $C H_{4}$ is an eight-electron process, the high selectivity suggests that it has higher electron density in hybrid structures than bare $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ . It may due to the enhanced efficiency in separating photogenerated electron-hole pairs in the hybrid system. Moreover, they concluded that the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ in $\cup_{3}(B T C)_{2}@{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ is firstly photoexcited to generate electron-hole pairs, and then the electrons are transferred to the $C\cup_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ . eantime, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorbed on the Cu sites of $C\cup_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ is reduced and the oxidation occurs on the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ . This work opens a door to design MOF photocatalysts for the photocatalytic conversion of $C O_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ .
Besides $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ nanoparticles were also immobilized onto the surface of MOF to boosting the activity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. For example, in 2019, Cabrero-Antonino et $a I$ .[148] designed a n-based MOF composite catalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ the methanation (Figure 19), $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{M O F}(\mathsf{Z n}){\cdot}1,$ by incorporating $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ nanoparticles into $\mathsf{M O F}(\mathsf{Z n})-1([\mathsf{Z n}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c a})_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}],\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t c a}=$ ,2,3-benzotriazole-5-carboxylic acid). The results showed that the incorporation of small $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ nanoparticles significantly promotes the catalytic activity of the resulting hybrid MOF with a TOF value of $50\times10^{-3}{\cdot}5^{-1}$ at $215^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which is much higher than reported for Ni@MIL-101(Cr) $(1.63\times10^{-3}{\cdot}5^{-1}$ at $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ .[149] The promoted activity can be attributed to the following factors: (1) The superior photoactivity of $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{o m o F}(\mathsf{Z n}){-}1$ under UV region provide intrinsic advantages for light adsorption; (2) The small
$\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ NPs behaving both as semiconductor and cocatalyst nearby the Zn reduction centers generate a synergistic effect; (3) The incorporation of $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ NPs increases the charge separation efficiency.
The composites of MOF and $g{-}C_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ can be also regarded as promising photocatalysts for conversing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ . In 2018, Xu et $a I.$ .[150] prepared the BIF $-20@\mathsf{g}-\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheet with high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction activity. BIF- $-20@\tt g-C_{3}N_{4}$ exhibits better catalytic performance with the high $C H_{4}$ evolution rate (15.524 $\mu\mathsf{m o l}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ than bare $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheet. The DFT calculation results demonstrated that the presence of B-H bonding in hybrid material is beneficial to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adhesion and activation during the reaction process.
To date, very limited investigations on the photocatalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ by MOFs have been performed. It remains a daunting challenge to improve the catalytic performance and selectivity of MOF based photocatalysts for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methane.
