Generally, the core problems of most photocatalysts remain to be solved include poor ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity, weak light adsorption, inability to efficiently generate photoexcited electrons, and/or poor transfer of the photo-generated electrons to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed on the catalyst surface.
## 3.2. MOFs for electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ reduction
Electrochemical carbon dioxide reduction reactions $(C O_{2}R R)$ , as a new method for the conversion form ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to other value-added chemicals, are always driven by renewable electricity under ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -saturated mild condition.[163-166] Although extensive efforts have been made in the development of electrochemical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{R R}},$ outstanding challenges are remaining: the poor selectivity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, low catalytic efficiency, high overpotential, and the stability of the catalyst.[167-168] Compared with other materials, MOFs not only provide redox-active sites and adsorb abundant ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with the unique surfaces and pores, but also promote the transport of protons, thereby enhancing the performance of electrochemical $C O_{2}R R$ .[169-171] In this section, we will conclude the electrocatalysis of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to carbon monoxide, methane, formic acid, and ethanol.[172,173]
