## 3.2.2. Conversion of $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ to liquid small-molecules
Some liquid molecules such as formate, acetate, methanol, ethanol, and oxalic acid could also be obtained by MOF electrocatalysts for conversing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Massive efforts have been devoted to the optimization of MOF-based electrocatalytic erformance. In this context, Cu-based MOF was found to be the ideal candidate for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electrocatalytic reduction due to it can uniquely produce hydrocarbons at high reaction rates over sustained periods of time.[179] Therefore, some Cu-based MOFs have emerged as electrocatalysts for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction in recent years.
In 2016, Albo and researchers[180] investigated the electrocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ using gas diffusion electrodes modified by Cu-based MOFs, including HKUST-1, CuAdeAce, and mesoporous metal-organic aerogel (MOA) of CuDTA and CuZnDTA (Figure 27a). The result showed that the as-prepared MOF and MOA exhibit high efficiency for the production of methanol and ethanol in the liquid phase. As shown in Figure 27b, the product distribution and process efficiency are correlated with the current density applied to the system. The HKUST-1 and CuZnDTA exhibit a higher faradaic efficiency (FE) of $15.9\%$ , and $9.9\%$ , respectively, compared with CuAdeAce $\prime F E=1.2\%)$ , CuDTA $(F E=6\%$ ) electrocatalysts. The lower FE values of CuAdeAce and CuDTA ould be attributed to steric hindrance from the surrounding ligands, affecting the accessibility of the pentacoordinate Cu(II) centers. Besides, the result showed that the HKUST-1-based and uZnDTA-based electrocatalysts exhibited significantly stable electrocatalytic performance for 17 and $12\mathsf{h}$ , respectively.
In 2017, Hupp and coworkers[181] incorporated copper nanoparticles into a thin film of NU-1000 for installing the single site Cu(II) into NU-1000 (Figure 28a). It is observed from Figure 28b that the hydrogen evolution covers the entire potential range and the production rate of hydrogen is enhanced with the increase of the overpotential. No ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction products are detectable at a voltage range of $-0.62\vee$ to $-0.72\vee$ vs. RHE. The FE of each product at each potential is shown in Figure 28c. The results showed that the FE for hydrogen evolution reaches a minimum of $66\%$ at an applied potential of $-0.82\vee.$ and the FE for formate production achieves a maximum of $28\%$ at this potential. A competition between $C O_{2}R R$ and HER was found in the catalyst, of which the selectivity toward $C O_{2}R R$ products should be further improved in the future.
