Most recently, in 2020, Lamagni et al.[183] prepared a bismuth-based MOF Bi(1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene), amely Bi(btb). The as-constructed Bi(btb) serves as a precatalyst. The precatalyst can eventually form Bi-based NPs dispersed in a porous organic matrix via the structural rearrangement (Figure 29). The results showed that the as-prepared MOF exhibits high selectivity over a broad potential range in the $C O_{2}R R$ toward formate production with a FE up to $95\%$ and a current density up o $261\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The high activity of catalysts could be attributed to the well-dispersed Bi nanoparticles with highly exposed active sites. This work offers a new approach to prepare metal-based materials with high activity, selectivity, and electronic conductivity for applications in electrocatalysis.
To date, the main problems of MOF-based electrocatalysts for $\supset_{2}$ conversion lie in low FE efficiency and poor product selectivity. This may due to the inherent drawbacks of poor conductivity for MOF as well as the competition between HER and $C D_{2}R R$ reaction. In this context, we believe that growing or coating MOF layers on highly conductive, porous, and rigid templates can provide an effective way to enhance the catalytic
performance for the MOF-based electro-catalysts. The creation of hydrophobic environment could be an effective way to suppress the HER reaction. Furthermore, the stability of catalysts should be further enhanced via molecular design and reaction control. Therefore, there is still a lot of work to do in designing highly efficient, low-cost MOF-based electrocatalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the application of MOF based catalysts in the conversion of carbon dioxide is reviewed in detail. MOF based catalysts have received wide attention due to their excellent features. The reported works have demonstrated outstanding properties of MOF based catalysts. This suggests that MOFs exhibit great potential in the conversion of carbon dioxide to many useful fine chemicals and small-molecule compounds.
Although MOF based catalysts had attracted increasing attention recently. However, some MOF based catalytic systems often use some expensive and environmentally unfriendly compounds as cocatalysts such as TBAB to improve their catalytic performance. Thus, some MOF catalysts system without the aid of expensive and noxious cocatalyst should be suitably designed and developed.
Since the advantages of rich porosity, stability, variable active sites, high surface area, adjustable structure, and easy functionalization, scientists have made great efforts to explore versatile potential applications of MOF-based catalysts for the conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ including conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to cyclic carbonate, alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters, carboxylic propargyl amine, carbon monoxide, methane, formate, acetate, etc. Even though these studies cover a wide range of the applications of MOF-based catalysts, the application of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion mainly focuses on chemically synthetic ways. The photocatalysis and electrocatalysis are still not well developed. Therefore, researchers should pay more attention to investigate the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to useful products through photocatalysis and electrocatalysis. Besides, the industrial application is not available due to the stability and large scale preparation of catalysts is still a challenging problem. Therefore, the researches on the practical applications of MOF catalysts should be studied in depth and systematization.
The development of MOF based catalysts provides a novel and popular methodology to adsorb $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and convert it into useful chemical raw materials. In the future, with the rapidly increasing atmospheric ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentrations, the fixation of carbon dioxide will become a key technology in decreasing greenhouse gas pollution. Since MOFs possesses excellent features, it will remain a key candidate in promoting the transformation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Furthermore, more in-depth researches are needed to extend the applied range of MOF based catalysts in the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to useful products. We believe that MOF based catalysts will promote the development of industrial production based on ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation.
